{
  "teamId": 114,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Cleveland Guardians opened the year with a familiar identity: a pitching-first club that would grind out wins even when the offense sputtered. March and April were a coin-flip at 16-16 combined, but the early calendar masked genuine drama. A rocky start from Gavin Williams and back-to-back bullpen meltdowns from Cade Smith threatened to bury Cleveland before the season found its legs. What saved April was the kind of homegrown heroism the Guardians have become known for — Daniel Schneemann's grand slam, a Brayan Rocchio three-run shot, and Chase DeLauter coming up clutch in consecutive late-inning spots reminded fans that this team never stopped competing.\n\nMay was the turning point that defined the season. Cleveland went 18-11, their best month by a wide margin, riding a suddenly locked-in rotation and a lineup that finally started doing damage. Kyle Manzardo's go-ahead blast in late May crystallized the feeling: this team had found something. June brought the inevitable regression — a 10-15 record haunted by more Cade Smith misfortune and a brutal late-game single that flipped a win into a loss — but the Guardians never fully collapsed. July and August were a collective tread-water at 25-26, yet Brayan Rocchio delivered a signature moment on July 2nd, a two-run home run that swung nearly 80 win probability points in Cleveland's favor, keeping the team in the thick of a tight American League race.\n\nSeptember became the month Brayan Rocchio announced himself as the heart of this ballclub. He homered twice in a single September 4th game, then delivered a stunning two-run double on September 15th — a .789 WPA swing — to essentially will the Guardians toward the postseason. Petey Halpin added a clutch three-run shot two days later when Cleveland needed it most, and the team closed 16-9 to finish 85-77 and punch their ticket to the ALDS. It was not a dominant regular season, but it was a resilient one, built on late-game toughness and the emergence of a core that refused to let a difficult June and July define them.\n\nNow the Guardians find themselves locked in a tense ALDS, the series knotted at 2-2 with everything on the line. Jo Adell has emerged as an unexpected postseason catalyst, delivering two clutch triples to spark Cleveland rallies, while José Ramírez reminded everyone why he remains the franchise's cornerstone with a go-ahead home run in Game 4. The bullpen has shown flashes of brilliance — a critical double play and a lineout to strand runners — and the defense has held when it had to. With the series tied and a winner-take-all game on the horizon, the Guardians' season now rests on a single game. Everything that came before was prologue.",
